== The Second Sermon

#quote[On this mountain the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts will make for all people a feast of fat things, a feast of wines on the lees, of fat things full of marrow, of wines on the lees well refined.—Isa 25:6.]

I have shown that Christ and his benefits are compared to a feast, and in what respects they are fitly likened to a feast, and have urged that we should prepare for it, first by getting large hearts. Now, in the second place, if we are to have comfort at this feast, we must labor for a spiritual appetite; for to what end and purpose is a man at a feast who has no stomach? I will therefore show what means we are to use to get eager stomachs and holy appetites for this feast.

(1.) The appetite is raised with sour things, such as anguish of spirit and mournfulness of heart for sin. If we are ever to relish Christ rightly, we must labor to have a lively apprehension of our sins. We must do as the Jews did at the Passover. They ate it with sour herbs, so that they might have the sharper stomachs. So must we. We must cast our eyes into our own hearts, and consider what vile wretches we are, how full of sin and vanity; and this will be as sour herbs to the Paschal Lamb. We must join the sweet benefits and privileges that we have in Christ with the consideration of our own wretched and miserable condition, and then this heavenly ordinance cannot help being sweet and comforting to our souls. I beg you#super[+], enter into your own souls, and consider seriously under what guilt you lie, and this will whet your appetite. “A full stomach despises the honeycomb” (Prov 27:7); but in this appetite there is a sense of emptiness, and from that sense of emptiness pain, and from pain an earnest desire for satisfaction. So it is in spiritual things. We need Christ, and all the spiritual comforts that flow from him. There is an emptiness in us, and we see a need every day to feed upon the mercies of God in Christ. There is an emptiness in our souls, and there must be a sense of that emptiness, and pain from that sense, which must stir up a strong endeavor to follow after what we desire. Then Christ is indeed sweet, when we find our souls hungering and thirsting after him.

(2.) Again, if we would have that appetite of spirit that is fit for this feast, we must purge our souls from the corruptions of flesh and spirit, “perfecting holiness in the fear of God” (2 Cor 7:1). We must cleanse our souls from the lusts and passions that daily cling to them. All undigested matter must be taken away, so that the edge of the stomach is not blunted: for while these earthly, carnal corruptions lie upon the soul, we can expect no spiritual appetite for heavenly things. Let us therefore examine ourselves, what filth lies upon our souls, and what corrupt inclinations are there, so that they may be purged, and our desires be carried fully after Christ in the sacrament.

(3.) Another means to get an appetite is to consider thoroughly what is required of a Christian to keep up the trade of Christianity well. It is a different kind of thing from what we take it for, to keep up communion with God, to perform holy duties in a holy manner, to bear the yoke as a Christian should. A great deal of strength is required here; and because corruptions will mix themselves into our best performances, there must be a great deal of mercy from God to pardon them. And where does all this come from but from the death of our blessed Savior Jesus Christ? For his sake, God has a forbearing eye. Now, if we consider what a degree of spiritual strength and vigor we should have to go through with these duties, this would sharpen our stomachs and spiritual appetites to furnish ourselves with grace from Christ to go through with these holy services. There must be an exercise of all the duties of Christianity, which is a state that must be kept up at a great deal of cost and labor. A man can do no service acceptable to God except by grace; and grace must feed the soul with fruitful knowledge in the power of faith. And when the soul feels a necessity of grace, oh! then, beloved, it hungers and earnestly thirsts after the love of God in Christ. Every trade needs a great deal of knowledge. Then surely the calling of Christianity needs a great deal. A Christian must expect much both in prosperity and adversity, as the apostle says, “I have learned to be in need and to abound, to be in honor and to be in disgrace, and I can do all things through Christ who strengthens me” (Phil 4:12). Now, because so much goes out for the upkeep of Christianity, we must also bring in much grace, and faith, and love, and holiness, or else we will never be able to uphold this condition. Where there is an exercise of Christianity, there will be an appetite for heaven; that is our best calling. For when we have done all that we can, what we must have comfort from is Christianity. Therefore, labor with all labor to be holy and able Christians. All other callings are only for this present life; but the one that is for eternity is this calling of Christianity. And this is only to fit us here in this world for an everlasting condition of glory in the world to come.

(4.) Again, if we would have a desire and appetite for heavenly things, we must labor to get acquainted, and constantly keep company, with those who are good. The old proverb is, “Company will make a man fall to,” especially the company of those who are better than ourselves. Out of sheer emulation, men will do as others do. When men live among those whose hearts are framed this way, they must keep level with them. Keeping company with those who have a good relish for spiritual things, and show forth grace in their lives, sets an appetite on our desires, to desire the same things that they do. So St Paul writes to the Gentiles to stir up the emulation of the Jews. Therefore take this also as a means of getting a spiritual appetite. To go on.

(5.) The next thing that may stir up our desires to get an appetite for the best things is to consider seriously that we cannot tell how long we have to live, or may enjoy the benefit of the means of grace. Those who sit at table and talk away the greatest part of dinner time need at last to eat all the faster, the more negligent they have been in eating before. We cannot tell how long we may enjoy this spiritual feast that God makes for us. Therefore, be stirred up to get spiritual appetites; for we do not know how long God will spread a table for us. We do not know how long we will enjoy our lives; and if we are taken by surprise, we may suffer a spiritual famine, a famine of the soul, if we have nothing beforehand to comfort us; and of all famines, a spiritual famine is the most grievous, the most fearful. Therefore do as Joseph did, and be wise. In the seven years of plenty he gathered for the seven years of famine that were to come upon the land of Egypt, Gen 41:36. Alas! if we have nothing laid up beforehand, what will our end be? We will lie open to God’s wrath and anger. Nothing can support our souls in the evil time. Therefore, as you#super[+] desire on that day to have comfort from the things you will stand most in need of, labor to get a good appetite. For to perish and starve at a feast is a shame; to famish in the liberty of the gospel and plenty of spiritual food is shameful and dishonorable. So you#super[+] see, beloved, not to enlarge on the point, how you may get such an appetite as is fit for such a holy feast: first, by getting a sense of sin; secondly, by seeing a necessity of Christ; thirdly, by purging out the lusts that lie upon the soul; fourthly, by keeping company with those who are spiritually minded; and lastly, by considering the time to come.

Use 3. It is not enough to have a stomach, but we must have a spiritual disposition of soul toward heavenly things, as we have toward outward things. Labor to have a taste for good things, and a taste that distinguishes heavenly things from other things. God is the God of nature, and has furnished us with five senses; and as he has given us sense to perceive, so he has furnished the creatures with varieties of excellences, suited to all our several senses. He will not have objects in the creatures without a sense, nor a sense in man without objects. He has furnished man with senses, and a variety of senses, and given fit and proportionate objects for those senses. The soul also has her sense. Wherever there is life, there is sense. God, having given spiritual life to the soul, maintains that life with spiritual food. In a feast there is sight, and the eye is fed there not only with rich furnishings but with a variety of dainties; the ear likewise and the smell are satisfied, the one with music, the other with sweet scents. So in this feast there is something to delight both the ear and the smell of the soul: the one with hearing the gracious promises of Jesus Christ, and the other in receiving the sweet fragrance of that sacrifice that was offered up once for all. Nothing is so sweet to the soul as the blessings of Christ. He is sweet in the word, as the vessel that conveys him into our souls. So you#super[+] see that in this feast all the senses, the sight, the smell, the taste, and hearing, are satisfied, and great care is taken in the provision for the feast that our outward man may be pleased. And will the Lord of Hosts make a feast, and not content the whole man? He is for our sight, if we have spiritual eyes to see; for the ear, if we have ears to hear. All the senses are exercised here. What is the reason why carnal men cannot relish a pardon for sin, and justification, and sanctification, and holiness, nor go boldly to God? It may be they have good, sweet notions of these, but they have no spiritual taste or relish of them, and all because they lack spiritual life. None but a Christian can have a spiritual taste answering to a spiritual life. Taste is a kind of feeling, one of the most necessary senses; and a Christian cannot be without relish and feeling. Indeed, it is the very being of a Christian to have a taste of spiritual things. Of all the senses, there is a stronger application in taste. The other senses fetch their objects from far off; but as for taste, there is a near application in it, and therefore it is the most necessary. Every life is maintained by taste. “Taste and see that the #smallcaps[Lord] is good” (Ps 34:8).

Now, taste does two things: it relishes what is good, and dislikes the contrary. There must be a spiritual taste to discern differences. There can be no spiritual taste unless it knows what is good and profitable for the soul, and what is not. Because God will not have our tastes wronged, you#super[+] see what course he takes. First, the eye sees what things we taste, and if the eye is displeased, so also is the smell. So God lays spiritual things before us, the knowledge of good and bad, and gives us many warnings, and all because he would not have us taste things hurtful to the soul, or poison instead of food. Now, when we have tasted what is good, let us take heed that it is not a taste only, lest we fall into the sin against the Holy Spirit.

Use 4. Again, besides taste, there must be a digesting of what we taste, and that thoroughly, in our understandings. When we apprehend a thing to be true and good, it must be digested thoroughly into the affections. Love for the best things must be above all other love whatever; yet this must be digested. Men often have sweet notions, but, alas! they are only notions; they do not digest them into their affections. It is the last digestion that nourishes; and when any spiritual truths are understood thoroughly, then spiritual strength comes in; and upon this the soul comes and sucks in the virtue that is for its nourishment. So it is in the soul; upon digestion there is nourishment.

Again, there must be a faculty to retain what we have received, so that it may be digested. There are many who love to hear, but they do not digest. If there is nothing in the soul, nothing can be drawn out; and therefore we must learn to retain necessary truths, so that on occasion they may come from the memory into the heart. Though indeed they are not in their proper place when they are in the memory only, yet if they are there, they may with ease be brought down into the soul.

Use 5. Then we must labor to walk in the strength of spiritual things. For what is the use of this feast but to cherish both soul and spirit? The use of the spiritual things we have through Christ is to cherish and enliven. It conveys strength to us, so that we may walk in the strength of Christ, as Elijah walked forty days in the strength of his food, 1 Kgs 19:8. And consider that, though in our consciences and conditions we have a variety of changes, yet in Christ we have several comforts suited to all our several conditions. If our sins trouble us, we should watch over ourselves, so that we are not overmuch cast down, but feed upon spiritual things in the consideration of pardon for sin in the blood of Christ. This is the great issue of all that Christ has traced out in the forgiveness of sins. He is not, he cannot be, divided. Where he pardons sins, he sanctifies; where he sanctifies, he writes his law in their hearts. So there is a chain of spiritual favors. Where the first link is, all the rest follow. Where forgiveness of sin is, there is the Spirit, and that Spirit sanctifies, and comforts, and is a pledge of everlasting life. Therefore, feed especially upon the favors of God, and get forgiveness of sins, and then all the rest of the chain of grace and spiritual life will follow.

Sometimes we stand in need of present grace and comfort, and we are undone if comforts and grace are not at hand, never considering the promises that are to come, such as that promise of Christ, “I will be with you to the end of the world; do not fear” (Matt 28:20). No temptation will befall us but we will have a way out of it, and it will work together for the good of all those who fear God. This is aqua vitae \[water of life\] to the soul of man. Therefore we should always remember to get the gracious promises of Christ and his Holy Spirit into our souls; for when all other comforts fail, then the comforts of the Spirit come in, who will be with us and uphold us in all extremities. If we had nothing in this world to comfort our spirits, yet let us rejoice in hope of the glory to come. “Our life is hidden with Christ” (Col 3:3). We have “the hidden manna” (Rev 2:17). “In him we rejoice in hope of glory” (Rom 5:2). And the way to maintain a Christian, holy life is to make use of all the privileges of Christianity, and of the promises that convey these privileges to our souls.

Now, so that we may do this better, let us observe continually what it is that hinders us from feeding upon spiritual things as we should. Whatever it is, we must labor constantly to remove it.

Now, what must follow after this feast? Why! spiritual cheerfulness.

If we find this in our duties of Christianity, it is a sign we have fed upon spiritual things. The nature of a spiritual feast is to empty the soul of sin, and to fill it full of gracious thoughts and actions. In place of that, it moderates all things. It makes us use the world as if we did not use it. When we can do this, we may certainly know that our souls have tasted an abundance of benefit by this feast.

A man who has no spiritual joy is for the most part drowned in the contentments of the world, drowned in riches and honors; and these are like strong drink taken without measure, which, instead of cheering the spirits, exhausts and kills them. He who has the joy of heaven here by faith is mortified to all other base delights; “he minds only the things above, where Christ is” (Col 3:1). And therefore the exhortation, or rather command, “Seek the things that are above,” has this promise fittingly attached to it, “and then all other things will be cast in upon you#super[+]” (Matt 6:33): riches and honors in the world; and if not those, yet as much as is necessary, and the mortification of our sins and the lusts of the flesh.

Again, if we have fed upon spiritual things for our souls, we will be thankful. The man who has tasted how good and gracious the Lord has been to him in this world, and how full of joy and comfort he will be to him in another world, cannot help, in consideration of this, being thankful to God in his soul.

Here we see how to make this spiritual food that Christ provides for us fit for our souls. And if there is such joy as we have said there is in spiritual things, what further use should we make of them but to labor from this to justify the ways of godliness against our own false and carnal hearts, and against the slanderous charges of the world? When our hearts are ready to be false to us, and hanker after the contentments of the world, and are ready to say that the best contentment they can enjoy is in the things below, let us answer our base and false arguing hearts, that the ways of wisdom, the ways that God directs us to, they alone are the ways of pleasure. And religion is what makes the hearts of the children of men joyful; and “only a good conscience makes a continual feast” (Prov 15:15), as long as a man lives. But especially at the hour of death, when all the comforts of the world cease, then conscience stands as our friend.

Objection. But the world’s objection is that, of all kinds of men in the world, those who profess religion are the most melancholy.

Answer. But if it is so, it is because they are not religious enough. Their sins are continually before their eyes. They have pardon for sin, and freedom from the guilt of sin, but do not know it. They have good things, and do not know them. And so, with regard to spiritual comforts, God’s people may have spiritual joy and inward consolation, and yet not know it. There may be a time when they are sad and droop, and that is when they think God does not look pleasantly upon them. But the true mark of a Christian is to be cheerful, and no one else can be truly cheerful and joyful. Joy is usurped by others. There is no comfort in them that can be called real. All the joy of a carnal man is only, as it were, the joy of a traitor. He may come to the sacraments, and feast with the rest of God’s people, but what mirth or joy can he have as long as the Master of the feast frowns upon him? Where Christ is not, there God is not reconciled. There is no joy like the joy of him who is assured of the love of God in Christ. A man may sometimes, through ignorance, lack the joy that belongs to him. “Rejoice, you#super[+] righteous, and be glad” (Ps 33:1). It belongs to those who are in Christ and to the righteous to rejoice, for joy is all their portion. They alone can justify the ways of God against all reproaches whatever. But the eyes of carnal men are so held in blindness that they can see no joy, no comfort in this course. As is said of Augustine before his conversion, he was afraid to become a Christian indeed, lest he should lack all the joys and pleasures that the world then afforded him; but after he was converted, then he could cry, “Lord, I have stayed away from you too long,” and too long delayed coming in to taste the sweetness of Jesus Christ.

Take a Christian at his worst, and he is better than another man taken at his best. The worst condition of God’s children far surpasses the very best condition of graceless persons. The outcome of things will turn to the good of him who is a member of Christ, a child of God, an heir of heaven. The evil of evils is taken away from him. Take him at his worst, he is an heir of heaven; but take the wicked man at his best, he is not a child of God, he is a stranger to God, he is like a branch cut off, and as miserable a wretch as ever Belshazzar was in the midst of his cups, trembling and quaking with fear and astonishment when he saw the writing on the wall, Dan 5:24. When a man apprehends the wrath of God hanging over his head, though he were at the greatest feast in the world, and among those who make mirth and jollity, yet the sight of vengeance ready to seize upon him cannot help dampening all his joy and all his carnal pleasures; and therefore only a Christian has a true title to this feast.

I beg you#super[+], let us labor earnestly to have our part and portion in the things above. But what are they to do who as yet see no interest of their own in Jesus Christ? Why! let them not be discouraged, for all are compelled to come into this feast, both blind and lame. The servants are sent to bring them in. God invites the most wretched people of all. All who are aware of their sins are called to come in to this feast; and that God requires of us, or else we can have no appetite to taste this feast. God says, “Come, all” (Isa 55:1). Yes, but, says the poor sinful soul, I have no grace at all! Why! come all the same, “buy without money”; the feast is free. “God’s thoughts are not as your thoughts are”; “but as heaven is high above the earth, so his thoughts are above your thoughts” (Isa 55:8, 9). Poor wretch! You think you have led a wicked life, and so you have! Yes, but now come in; God has invited you, and he will not always be inviting you. Therefore come in, and study the excellencies of Christ. When such persons as these see that they need mercy, and grace, and reconciliation, and must either have it or else be damned forever, now they are earnest to study the favor and love of God in Christ; now they bestir themselves to get peace of conscience and joy in the Holy Spirit; now they see salvation to be founded only on Christ, and all the other excellencies belonging to Christianity; and therefore such a man goes constantly provided with grace and holiness in this life, so that he may not lose his part in glory in the life to come. Think of this and pray for it, as they did in the gospel: “Lord, always give us this bread” (John 6:34). Here is hope that you may be saved, because you are invited to come in. To what end is the ministry of the gospel, but to entreat you to be reconciled? Oh! let this work upon our souls when we hear of the excellencies of these things! And together with them, let us consider the necessity that is laid upon us to obtain them, and that we must have them or else be damned eternally. We must do as the lepers did, who said to one another, “Why just sit here until we die? If we say, ‘Let us go into the city,’ we will die there from the famine in the city; but if we sit here, we will also die.” Now, what course did they take? “They said to one another, Let us go over to the camp of the Arameans; there is food to feed us” (2 Kgs 7:3, 4). So the soul says, If I go into the city of the world, I will be starved there; if I sit still, I will also perish. What shall I do now? I will venture upon Jesus Christ; he has food that endures to eternal life, and if I perish there, I perish. If I do not have Christ I must die; the wrath of God hangs over my head, and I cannot escape. Alas! poor soul, now that you see your wretchedness, cast yourself upon him, and come in. If you venture, you can but die! Venture therefore, put yourself upon God’s mercy, for he is gracious and full of compassion.

Those who have given themselves up to Christ, let them study to honor God and Christ by taking the comforts that are allotted to them. When any man invites us to a feast, he knows that if we respect him we will fall to. God has bestowed his Son upon us, and will he not with him give us all things? Let us not therefore dishonor the bounty of our good God, but come in, and labor to have our hearts more and more enlarged with the consideration of the excellency of these eternal comforts. The fullness of Christ is able to satisfy the soul, though it were a thousand times larger than it is. If it were possible for us to get the capacity of angels, it could not be enough to show forth the fullness of pleasures that are provided for a Christian. Let us therefore labor with all labor to open our hearts to receive these joys, for we cannot honor God more than by receiving thankfully from his bounty what he freely offers. To taste plentifully, in the covenant of grace, of these riches, and joy, and hope of things to come, glorious above all that we are able to think of: this, I say, is the way to honor God under the gospel of hope. Of things that are infinite, the more we take, the more we may take, and the more we honor him who gives. Let us therefore enter deeply into our particular sins; there is no fear of despair. Think of all your needs, and of all your sins; however many they are, yet there is more to be had in Christ than can be lacking in you. The soul that thinks itself full of needs is the richest soul, and the soul that sees no lack at all, no need of grace or Christ, is always sent away empty. Grieve therefore for your sins, and then rejoice that you have grieved, and go to God for the supply of all your needs. The seeds of joy and of comfort are sown in tears and grief in this world; but we know that we will reap in joy in the world to come.

Remember this: we have we know not what to go through in this valley of tears. That speech of Barzillai was good and excellent, who, being invited to the court by David himself, answered, “I have now grown old, I am not fit for the court, for my senses are decayed and gone” (2 Sam 19:35). Even so the time will come when our sense for relishing earthly pleasures will be utterly lost. We are sure to go to our graves, and we do not know what particular trouble we may meet with and go through in this world, if we live to a full age. Alas! what are all the comforts here to the comforts of eternity? When our days on earth are spent, then comes the eternity of pleasure or everlasting sorrow. Oh then, if, when we leave all behind us, we have the joy of the Holy Spirit in our hearts, it will lift us above all the suggestions of sin or Satan, and bring us cheerfully up to the judgment seat of Christ. Labor therefore to have a spiritual relish of soul, to grow in grace and the comforts of the Holy Spirit; for the time will come when we will wish that we had had more than we have. Everyone will repent of looseness and slackness in the ways of holiness. Therefore let us labor earnestly to be good stewards for our souls for the time to come.
